 "annotations": {
  "destructiveHint": false,
  "idempotentHint": false,
  "openWorldHint": false,
  "readOnlyHint": false,
  "title": "Add panel"
 },
 "description": "Add a panel to a board. Each panel answers one question and reads at a glance: a number, a line or a bar. Build it from the structured types; `sql` is the last resort. Choose in this order: `funnel` for conversion through ordered events (viewed pricing \u2192 started checkout \u2192 paid), with a breakdown for conversion per segment (per shop, per plan): always a funnel, never a query or trend lines per step. `retention` for whether people come back. `trends` for everything that counts or sums events: `chart` \"scalar\" for one number (revenue, installs, failed charges; several numbers are several scalar panels, never one wide table), \"line\" for a metric over time, \"bar\" to compare segments with a breakdown; set `display.format` to \"currency\" for money and \"percent\" for a rate. A table only when the rows themselves are the point (the operations or shops to look at), with at most four columns. `issues` for the top errors, `text` for a heading over a row of panels. `sql` only when none of those can show it (a ratio between two measures), with at most four columns, the board's range as `{from}`/`{to}` rather than now(), and `sqlReason` saying why. A funnel and retention count the project's identified users: if the people in the question are not them, say so in a text panel instead. The spec is exactly what query_trends, query_funnel, query_retention or run_sql just ran (same field names), so check the numbers with that tool first. The panel is run once before it is saved: one that fails or breaks these rules is refused and nothing is saved, so follow the message and call again. The board is a grid: a panel joins the last row when it is the same kind and there is room (numbers up to four to a row, charts two, a table or text on its own), and every row shares its width equally at one height. Add panels in the order they should read; to change the rows afterwards, use update_board with `rows`.",
